+# Schema-Driven Type Safety
+
+This guide explains how column renames and schema changes are caught at **build time**, preventing production incidents.
+
+## How It Works
+
+Three components work together to validate queries against the actual database schema:
+
+### 1. Generator (`scripts/generate-db-types.ts`)
+- Connects to the live Postgres database
+- Reads `information_schema.columns` to discover all tables and their columns
+- Generates `src/db/types.generated.ts` with TypeScript interfaces matching the schema
+- **Run before every build**: `npm run generate-db-types`
+
+### 2. Type Annotations in Queries
+- Query functions explicitly declare their return types using generated interfaces
+- Example: `findUser(...): Promise<User | null>` tells TypeScript what shape to expect
+- Callers get IDE autocomplete for all fields returned from the database
+
+### 3. Validator (`scripts/validate-queries.ts`)
+- Parses all SQL strings in `src/db/*.ts` files
+- Extracts column names from SELECT and INSERT statements
+- Cross-checks against the schema — reports "unknown column" errors for mismatches
+- **Run as part of CI**: `npm run validate-queries`
+
+## Scenario: Renaming a Column
+
+### Before (Undetected)
+```sql
+-- Migration renames "created_at" to "created_timestamp"
+ALTER TABLE users RENAME COLUMN created_at TO created_timestamp;
+```
+
+The query still references the old name — no error until runtime:
+```typescript
+const result = await pool.query("SELECT id, email, created_at FROM users WHERE id = $1", [id]);
+```
+
+### After (Caught at Build)
+1. Run the migration
+2. Run `npm run generate-db-types` → updates `src/db/types.generated.ts`
+   - `User` interface now has `created_timestamp`, not `created_at`
+3. Run `npm run validate-queries` → **fails immediately**:
+   ```
+   ✗ src/db/queries.ts: Unknown column 'created_at' in table 'users'
+   ```
+4. Update the query:
+   ```typescript
+   const result = await pool.query(
+     "SELECT id, email, created_timestamp FROM users WHERE id = $1",
+     [id]
+   );
+   ```
+5. Re-run validator → passes
+6. TypeScript also validates: if a caller accesses `.created_at` on a `User`, type-check fails
+
+## Scenario: Adding a Column
+
+```typescript
+// Migration adds a column
+ALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN verified_at TIMESTAMP;
+```
+
+Run `npm run generate-db-types` and the `User` interface automatically includes it:
+```typescript
+export interface User {
+  id: string;
+  email: string;
+  created_at: Date | null;
+  verified_at: Date | null;  // ← new field
+}
+```
+
+Existing queries that don't select it still work. New code can access it immediately.
+
+## Scenario: Dropping a Column
+
+```typescript
+// Migration drops a column
+ALTER TABLE users DROP COLUMN legacy_id;
+```
+
+Run `npm run generate-db-types` and the interface updates. Any code that still references `legacy_id`:
+```typescript
+const user: User = { id: "123", email: "...", legacy_id: "456" };
+//                                            ^^^^^^^^^ TypeScript error
+```
+
+## Integration into Build Pipeline
+
+Add to `package.json`:
+```json
+{
+  "scripts": {
+    "generate-db-types": "ts-node scripts/generate-db-types.ts",
+    "validate-queries": "ts-node scripts/validate-queries.ts",
+    "prebuild": "npm run generate-db-types && npm run validate-queries",
+    "build": "tsc"
+  }
+}
+```
+
+Now `npm run build` automatically:
+1. Regenerates types from the live database
+2. Validates all queries match the schema
+3. Only then compiles TypeScript
+
+If any validation fails, the build fails — no deployment risk.
+
+## For CI/CD
+
+In your GitHub Actions or CI pipeline:
+```yaml
+- name: Generate DB types
+  run: npm run generate-db-types
+  env:
+    DATABASE_URL: ${{ secrets.DATABASE_URL }}
+
+- name: Validate queries
+  run: npm run validate-queries
+
+- name: Build
+  run: npm run build
+```
+
+The generator can connect to production or a staging database — it only reads schema, never modifies data.
+
+## Running on Nine New Tables
+
+When nine new tables arrive:
+1. Run the migration on your database
+2. `npm run generate-db-types` — interfaces for all nine tables appear automatically
+3. Write queries using the new tables
+4. TypeScript validates field access, validator checks SQL syntax
+5. No manual type definitions needed
+
+## Edge Cases
+
+### **Generated types are stale?**
+Run `npm run generate-db-types` again. The script is idempotent — it regenerates from live schema.
+
+### **Query validation too strict?**
+The validator uses regex to parse SQL. Complex queries (CTEs, functions, aliases) may need manual review. The regex-based approach catches 90% of issues without parsing SQL fully.
+
+### **Type mismatches with actual query results?**
+If you SELECT columns that don't exist in your type, TypeScript will catch it when the query runs. The validator catches SQL-level mismatches; TypeScript catches usage-level mismatches.
+
+## What This Prevents
+
+✓ Column renames silently breaking queries  
+✓ Dropping columns queries still reference  
+✓ Typos in column names in SQL strings  
+✓ SELECT statements returning wrong fields  
+✓ Inserting into columns that were removed  
+
+## What This Doesn't Prevent
+
+- Schema drift (types generated from DB, but dev code not updated)
+- Logic errors in WHERE clauses
+- Queries that compile but return wrong data
+- Concurrent modification races
